  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe forecast on Sunday is a bit more complicated due to the northern \ntier shortwave mentioned above. This disturbance is expected to push \na weak cold front into the region on Sunday. This will not only \nusher slightly cooler temperatures into the area, but also bring a \nchance for afternoon showers and storms. At this time, it's \nuncertain just how far south the front will make it, but it appears \nlikely that it will be draped somewhere across the Mid-Mississippi \nValley, resulting in dramatically hotter conditions across our south \nthan our north. At this point, it appears more likely than not that \nthe vast majority of our area currently under a heat headline will \nremain quite hot through Sunday, so I've extended the headlines out \nthrough Sunday evening. The exception is some of our NE MO\ncounties, where it looks like the cold front may bring some \nrelief on Sunday. \n\nA more noticeable change to the sensible weather for the vast \nmajority of the CWA will begin on Monday. First of all, most \nguidance has Sunday's cold front stalling out in the vicinity \nof our southern CWA, so we should be starting the day a little \ncooler than what we've seen lately. Additionally, the upper \nlevel winds will become more northwesterly in response to a \nstout shortwave diving across south-central Canada. Weak cold \nair advection along these winds will help keep temperatures more\nseasonal for this time of year. The above shortwave will also \nbring a much stronger cold front through the region sometime on \nWednesday. The NBM is currently favoring a slower solution, \npushing temperatures back above normal on Wednesday. Both \nfrontal passages (Sunday and Wednesday) will also bring a chance\nfor showers and thunderstorms to the region, though at this \ntime, there is no strong evidence that the environment will be \nsupportive for strong to severe storms.",
